PART 4: DESIGNING WITH AUTOMATED TOOLS
4.1 Automated Design Logic
For each pair of zones:
1.	The system identifies all pipes connecting the two zones.
2.	Pipes are sorted by your selected priority attribute.
3.	The number of pipes you specify remains open.
4.	If “Highest” is selected, the largest values remain open.
5.	If “Lowest” is selected, the smallest values remain open.
6.	All others are closed.
This generates a complete zone configuration instantly.
________________________________________
4.2 Example – Designing for Redundancy
Goal: Each zone pair should have three connections, prioritizing the largest diameter pipes.
Settings:
•	Open connections = 3
•	Prioritize by = Diameter
•	Selection = Highest
•	Reset before applying = Enabled
Result: The three largest pipes for each zone pair remain open.
________________________________________
4.3 Example – Designing for Isolation
Goal: Completely isolate two zones.
Settings:
•	Open connections = 0
Result: No open connections exist between the selected zone pair.
________________________________________
4.4 Example – Designing for Efficiency
Goal: Keep only the shortest connections.
Settings:
•	Prioritize by = Length
•	Selection = Lowest
•	Open connections = 1
Result: Only the shortest pipe remains open between each zone pair.
________________________________________
4.5 Mixed Design Approach
1.	Use automated tools to generate a baseline.
2.	Enable Interactive Mode.
3.	Manually override specific pipes.
4.	Test and refine.
This approach combines efficiency and engineering judgment.
